<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  W3C SCXML 5.10 + B.2: an event a HOST injects carries a payload, and the
  document reads it as a value rather than as text.

  This is the one direction nothing in the repository asked. Every integration
  fixture drives its machine with `raise_external(event, "", "")` — measured
  2026-08-16, the payload argument is the empty string in every call on every
  channel — so the whole host-to-datamodel edge was covered by nothing. The
  W3C suite does not reach it either: its payloads originate INSIDE the
  document (`<send><content>`, `<param>`, `<donedata>`), which is a different
  code path that each backend implements separately.

  It is also the edge a supervising host actually uses. `examples/ai_loop`
  answers its machine with `{"done":true}` and the document selects on
  `_event.data.done`; the same shape reaches SCE from any embedder that has a
  JSON payload and a state machine to give it to.

  Three claims, three clauses, and a `<final>` for each failure so a red says
  which one broke rather than only that something did:

    settled    all three — a JSON object arrived as an object, text as text,
               and a payload shaped like an expression as that text
    mangled    the JSON payload did not become addressable properties
    garbled    the plain-text payload did not arrive as that string
    evaluated  `2 + 3` arrived as 5 — the payload was RUN

  The third is the one that separates reading the payload from running it,
  and it is written to be decisive in both languages this datamodel is
  lowered into: `2 + 3` is an expression in Lua and in JavaScript alike, and
  evaluating it gives 5 in both. §scxml-B-2-8-1 gives the payload three
  readings — XML becomes a DOM, JSON becomes the value, anything else becomes
  a space-normalized string — and text that merely looks like arithmetic is
  the third of those. Measured 2026-08-17, five of six backends took a fourth
  reading: they ran the text first, so this event landed in `evaluated` on
  Rust, Go, Python, C11 and Kotlin's QuickJS engine, and in `settled` on cpp
  and Kotlin's Rhino. One document, two answers, and no test asked either.

  The first guard tests `_event.data` before indexing it. That is not defensive
  style: a backend that drops the payload leaves the variable nil, and
  `nil.milestone` raises where `undefined.milestone` would — the run would then
  be reporting an evaluation error rather than the missing payload, and W3C
  §5.9.1 (a cond that raises is false) would send it to the same place with a
  different reason. Short-circuiting keeps the answer about the payload.

  `hold the line` starts with a letter on purpose: cpp `EventDataHelper::
  jsonStringToScriptValue` attempts a JSON parse only for bodies that open
  with a JSON-eligible character, so a word-initial payload exercises the
  raw-string fallback rather than the parser.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
       name="event_data_arrives_as_sent" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="waiting">

  <state id="waiting">
    <transition event="payload"
                cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.milestone === 'refined' &amp;&amp; _event.data.turns === 2"
                target="heard"/>
    <transition event="payload" target="mangled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="heard">
    <transition event="note" cond="_event.data === 'hold the line'" target="quoted"/>
    <transition event="note" target="garbled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="quoted">
    <transition event="arith" cond="_event.data === '2 + 3'" target="settled"/>
    <transition event="arith" target="evaluated"/>
  </state>

  <final id="settled"/>
  <final id="mangled"/>
  <final id="garbled"/>
  <final id="evaluated"/>
</scxml>
